WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.234
Welcome to Building Defender XDR,

2
00:00:02.234 --> 00:00:06.212
a developer workbook in the
Federal Modernization Workbook.

3
00:00:06.212 --> 00:00:09.200
This is Chapter 6: detections as code.

4
00:00:09.200 --> 00:00:12.159
A rule that can't be shown
to fire isn't a control,

5
00:00:12.159 --> 00:00:16.987
and a rule nobody can trace to a reviewed
file isn't a change anyone approved.

6
00:00:16.987 --> 00:00:22.042
We'll start from Learn's create, list and
update calls for custom detection rules,

7
00:00:22.042 --> 00:00:24.798
read the repository
layout, the validation,

8
00:00:24.798 --> 00:00:29.775
the tests against each rule's windows, and
the deployment plan, and walk two labs.

9
00:00:29.775 --> 00:00:32.137
Publishing the plan is Chapter 9's.

10
00:00:32.137 --> 00:00:35.006
The chapter has been
reviewed and approved;

11
00:00:35.006 --> 00:00:38.700
the code is illustrative,
tested against fixtures.

12
00:00:42.450 --> 00:00:44.212
Why treat detections as code?

13
00:00:44.212 --> 00:00:45.250
Two reasons.

14
00:00:45.250 --> 00:00:48.163
A rule that can't be shown
to fire isn't a control.

15
00:00:48.163 --> 00:00:50.685
And a rule that nobody can trace back to a

16
00:00:50.685 --> 00:00:53.513
reviewed file isn't a
change anyone approved.

17
00:00:53.513 --> 00:00:57.382
By the end of this chapter you can
keep custom detections as files,

18
00:00:57.382 --> 00:01:01.466
prove each one fires on its known-bad
event and stays quiet otherwise,

19
00:01:01.466 --> 00:01:05.263
and produce a deployment plan that
an approver signs by its hash.

20
00:01:05.263 --> 00:01:09.401
It's for the developers and the
detection engineer who owns this gate.

21
00:01:09.401 --> 00:01:14.348
The rule lifecycle itself, author,
test, alert-only, promote and review,

22
00:01:14.348 --> 00:01:20.620
is the Security Operations book's; this
chapter builds its test and its deployment.

23
00:01:24.370 --> 00:01:27.520
Microsoft Learn's Graph
pages give three calls.

24
00:01:27.520 --> 00:01:32.209
Post to create a rule, which needs read-write
custom detection and the Detection

25
00:01:32.209 --> 00:01:35.407
tuning manage permission,
or Security Administrator;

26
00:01:35.407 --> 00:01:38.320
the quota is ten creates
a minute per application.

27
00:01:38.320 --> 00:01:41.157
Get to list the rules,
with the read permission.

28
00:01:41.157 --> 00:01:45.457
And patch to update one, supplying
only the properties that change.

29
00:01:45.457 --> 00:01:49.308
A rule has a query, a schedule,
a status of enabled or disabled,

30
00:01:49.308 --> 00:01:52.294
and a detection action
with an alert template,

31
00:01:52.294 --> 00:01:56.145
and optionally automated
actions, like isolating devices.

32
00:01:56.145 --> 00:01:59.735
None of these pages has
a national-cloud table,

33
00:01:59.735 --> 00:02:03.608
so the API's GCC availability
is not yet specified.

34
00:02:03.608 --> 00:02:07.630
The guide's interim path is
the portal, from the same file.

35
00:02:11.380 --> 00:02:12.380
What's left to you?

36
00:02:12.380 --> 00:02:16.555
Nothing checks a rule before it reaches
the tenant, so the kit validates it.

37
00:02:16.555 --> 00:02:21.667
Automated actions in the same body could
let a rule isolate devices on its own,

38
00:02:21.667 --> 00:02:24.942
outside the response ladder,
so such a rule is held.

39
00:02:24.942 --> 00:02:30.343
One test window can't show a rule is both
sensitive and quiet, so each rule has two.

40
00:02:30.343 --> 00:02:34.813
The repository and the tenant
drift, so the kit plans: create,

41
00:02:34.813 --> 00:02:39.192
update only what changed, hold
what failed, report orphans.

42
00:02:39.192 --> 00:02:42.981
A file marked enabled might never
have been tested, so it's held.

43
00:02:42.981 --> 00:02:46.930
And an approved plan could change
afterwards, so it carries a hash.

44
00:02:46.930 --> 00:02:52.450
The verdict: the API deploys anything it's
given; build the gate in front of it.

45
00:02:56.200 --> 00:02:59.937
The repository is one folder
with two files per rule.

46
00:02:59.937 --> 00:03:04.063
The rule file is the Graph request
body, in Learn's example shape.

47
00:03:04.063 --> 00:03:07.276
Beside it, a test file names
the tables the query reads,

48
00:03:07.276 --> 00:03:10.489
a known-bad window that must
return at least some rows,

49
00:03:10.489 --> 00:03:13.849
and a known-good window that
must stay under a threshold.

50
00:03:13.849 --> 00:03:16.175
The detection check does four things.

51
00:03:16.175 --> 00:03:19.574
Validates each rule, including
no automated actions,

52
00:03:19.574 --> 00:03:22.587
and that the query starts
from a declared table.

53
00:03:22.587 --> 00:03:27.565
Tests each rule's query over both
windows, through the hunting API.

54
00:03:27.565 --> 00:03:29.500
Reads the deployed rules.

55
00:03:29.500 --> 00:03:33.403
And plans: create, update
only the changed properties,

56
00:03:33.403 --> 00:03:38.190
hold with the reason, and report
orphans, never deleting them.

57
00:03:41.940 --> 00:03:42.990
Who's involved?

58
00:03:42.990 --> 00:03:45.644
The detection engineer owns this gate,

59
00:03:45.644 --> 00:03:50.453
writes each rule and its test file,
and reviews every rule each quarter.

60
00:03:50.453 --> 00:03:54.402
The approver reads the plan and
records its hash in the change record.

61
00:03:54.402 --> 00:03:59.704
And the security operations center replays
each known-bad event on a ring 0 device,

62
00:03:59.704 --> 00:04:04.227
under a change record, as the guide's
synthetic events table describes.

63
00:04:04.227 --> 00:04:07.640
One caution: advanced
hunting keeps thirty days.

64
00:04:07.640 --> 00:04:12.600
A known-bad window older than that returns
nothing, so keep the replay current.

65
00:04:16.350 --> 00:04:18.450
Lab 1, in the kit's folder.

66
00:04:18.450 --> 00:04:21.687
Step 1 reads one rule's
test file: one table,

67
00:04:21.687 --> 00:04:27.400
a known-bad day that must return a row,
and a quiet day that must return none.

68
00:04:27.400 --> 00:04:30.300
Step 2 validates, tests and plans.

69
00:04:30.300 --> 00:04:35.300
The admin-share rule fails validation:
it would isolate devices on its own.

70
00:04:35.300 --> 00:04:39.072
It also fails its quiet day,
three rows where one is allowed,

71
00:04:39.072 --> 00:04:41.037
so it would have been noisy too.

72
00:04:41.037 --> 00:04:43.738
The plan creates the LSASS dump-tool rule.

73
00:04:43.738 --> 00:04:47.469
It updates only the status of
the encoded PowerShell rule,

74
00:04:47.469 --> 00:04:52.288
which is deployed disabled and now
enabled in its file after a passing test.

75
00:04:52.288 --> 00:04:54.112
It holds the admin-share rule.

76
00:04:54.112 --> 00:04:59.780
And it reports a legacy rule, made in
the portal and in no file, as an orphan.

77
00:05:03.530 --> 00:05:06.862
Step 3 reads the plan an
approver signs: its hash,

78
00:05:06.862 --> 00:05:11.305
and the two actions, a post that
creates and a patch that updates.

79
00:05:11.305 --> 00:05:14.348
The update's body holds the
status and nothing else,

80
00:05:14.348 --> 00:05:17.318
because Learn's patch
supplies only what changes.

81
00:05:17.318 --> 00:05:22.993
Step 4 runs the same repository on the
interim path, with deploy via set to portal.

82
00:05:22.993 --> 00:05:26.649
The same plan comes out, as a
sheet for the Defender portal,

83
00:05:26.649 --> 00:05:28.517
each rule with its file's hash.

84
00:05:28.517 --> 00:05:30.950
Delete what the lab made when you're done.

85
00:05:34.700 --> 00:05:37.787
Lab 2 uses a test
tenant, never production.

86
00:05:37.787 --> 00:05:41.467
First make the known-bad event:
on a ring 0 test device,

87
00:05:41.467 --> 00:05:45.488
under a change record, run the
activity your rule detects.

88
00:05:45.488 --> 00:05:49.126
Then sign in with read scopes
only, and run the detection check

89
00:05:49.126 --> 00:05:53.050
with your own rule folder, its
known-bad window covering the replay.

90
00:05:53.050 --> 00:05:56.112
The test file should show
the known-bad window passing.

91
00:05:56.112 --> 00:05:59.575
If listing the deployed rules
fails in the test tenant,

92
00:05:59.575 --> 00:06:03.500
record it in the verification
log and set deploy via to portal.

93
00:06:03.500 --> 00:06:06.087
Nothing was deployed,
so you just sign out.

94
00:06:06.087 --> 00:06:09.870
Publishing comes in Chapter
9, with an approved hash.

95
00:06:13.620 --> 00:06:16.945
Here's what this gate proves,
against NIST's catalog.

96
00:06:16.945 --> 00:06:21.176
Each rule firing on its known-bad
event and staying quiet otherwise

97
00:06:21.176 --> 00:06:25.558
supports system monitoring and audit
review, analysis and reporting.

98
00:06:25.558 --> 00:06:28.063
Each change coming from a reviewed file,

99
00:06:28.063 --> 00:06:31.858
in a plan with a hash, supports
configuration change control.

100
00:06:31.858 --> 00:06:36.821
And what's deployed matching the repository,
with orphans named, supports both.

101
00:06:36.821 --> 00:06:40.008
The indicators are FedRAMP's,
as the guide maps them.

102
00:06:40.008 --> 00:06:44.180
The records support these controls;
the assessor decides them.

103
00:06:47.930 --> 00:06:49.330
What breaks in production?

104
00:06:49.330 --> 00:06:52.411
A rule with automated
isolation could contain a

105
00:06:52.411 --> 00:06:55.492
server or an emergency
account by query; it's held.

106
00:06:55.492 --> 00:06:58.694
A rule enabled without a
test is silent or noisy;

107
00:06:58.694 --> 00:07:01.404
it's held unless its test passed this run.

108
00:07:01.404 --> 00:07:05.068
A rule made in the portal can't
be traced; it's an orphan.

109
00:07:05.068 --> 00:07:07.878
A plan changed after
approval means the approver

110
00:07:07.878 --> 00:07:10.756
signed something else;
publishing checks the hash.

111
00:07:10.756 --> 00:07:15.755
A renamed column makes a rule stop
firing; the weekly test run catches it.

112
00:07:15.755 --> 00:07:21.950
And if the API isn't available in GCC,
the portal path deploys the same files.

113
00:07:25.700 --> 00:07:27.050
Here's what done looks like.

114
00:07:27.050 --> 00:07:32.000
Every rule has a test file, with both
windows inside the last thirty days.

115
00:07:32.000 --> 00:07:34.237
No rule has automated actions.

116
00:07:34.237 --> 00:07:37.688
Every enabled rule passed its
known-bad test in this run.

117
00:07:37.688 --> 00:07:42.662
Every orphan has a decision: into the
repository, or disabled from a file.

118
00:07:42.662 --> 00:07:45.399
And the plan's hash is
recorded with the approval.

119
00:07:45.399 --> 00:07:50.204
Next, Chapter 7 sets what Defender may
do on its own once these rules fire,

120
00:07:50.204 --> 00:07:54.113
and joins identity and cloud-app
signals to the endpoint's.

121
00:07:54.113 --> 00:07:55.270
Thanks for watching.
